            <section>
               <title value="Hospital course note"/>
               <code>
                  <coding>
                     <system value="http://loinc.org"/>
                     <code value="8648-8"/>
                     <display value="Hospital course note"/>
                  </coding>
               </code>
               <text>
                  <status value="generated"/>
                  <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Dear Colleague, I am writing to inform you regarding our shared patient, an 85-year-old male admitted on 21/06/1999 due to an acute exacerbation of his known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. On admission, the patient presented with increased dyspnoea, productive cough, and reduced oxygen saturation. Clinical examination, chest X-ray, arterial blood gas analysis, and pulse oximetry confirmed the exacerbation. Spirometry supported the diagnosis of severe airflow obstruction. The patient was treated with nebulised bronchodilators, systemic corticosteroids, supplemental oxygen therapy, and intravenous fluids. Vital signs were monitored closely throughout the stay. The patient responded well to treatment with improvement in respiratory status and oxygenation. He was discharged on 22/06/1999 in stable condition with inhaler therapy and a short oral steroid course. Patient education regarding inhaler technique and exacerbation self-management was provided. Please arrange close outpatient follow-up. Yours sincerely, Dr. [Treating Physician]</div>
               </text>
